From: z060142 <z060142@yahoo.com.tw>
Date: Mon, 31 Aug 2026 10:53:44 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 117/119] perf: the wave glare drew one sprite per bright
 texel, and brightness decides how many there are

The reported "brightness up, frame rate down" is a population problem. ExecuteDiffraction emits
one quad per half-resolution texel and the vertex shader collapses the empty ones, so the
rasterized sprite count is EXACTLY the number of texels above HDR_Streaks_Threshold - and under
the scene-referred pre-exposure that threshold is an absolute scene value. A bright sky puts tens
of thousands of texels over it at once, each drawing a sprite some 160 px across, six spectral
taps deep. At 1080p with Reach 0.15 that is 1 300 Mpx of blending against 0.24 Mpx for a single
night practical.

It is not the right picture either: the convolution of a broad bright region with the PSF is a
veil, not fifty thousand stars. The stars only exist because the deposit is done by point-sampled
sprites.

r_HDRDiffractionMaxSources (default 256, 0 = unlimited, scene-referred path only) caps it. Three
small passes - HDRDiffractionSourceStats, HDRDiffractionStatsReduce, HDRDiffractionBudget -
reduce the thresholded source to one RGBA32F texel holding (cutoff, raw count, count above the
cutoff, share of the excess below it), the last of them correcting the cutoff from the previous
frame's count with a multiplicative controller: fast up, slow down, a dead band at [0.8N, N], and
a 1x1 ping-pong because a pass may not read the target it writes. No CPU round trip anywhere.

The sprite vertex shader reads that one texel and fades a source out over one STOP. Below the
cutoff it draws no sprite but emits a ONE-TEXEL quad carrying the whole energy the pattern would
have spread (excess * gain * the deposited glare fraction) into the same layer, at the source's
own position. A source that loses the budget loses its SHAPE, not its light: turning the budget
on cannot darken a scene, and the crossfade means nothing pops while panning.

Byte identity is structural, not measured. raw <= N drives the cutoff to zero and a zero cutoff
is the un-budgeted vertex shader exactly; and the whole thing is gated on IsSceneReferredStage(5),
so off the switch DiffractionParams3.x is 0 and the branch is never entered.

Measure it, do not guess it. r_HDRDiffractionDebug 3's once-a-second report now LEADS with the
population, raw count first, and the stats chain runs whenever that mode is on even with the
budget at 0 - so a scene's real population can be read before deciding what N should be:

  [BLOOM]   SOURCES: raw above threshold = 51234 of the 960 x 540 grid -- one sprite each,
            162 x 162 half-res px per sprite = 1344.6 Mpx of blending unbudgeted
  [BLOOM]            budget 256 (r_HDRDiffractionMaxSources): 261 drew a star, cutoff 2.9 =
            +4.0 stops -- the other 50973 kept their LIGHT (12.4% of the source excess) and
            lost their shape

The counts are the GPU's own arithmetic over that frame's source and the raw test is literally
the vertex shader's collapse test. The 1x1 travels back on the existing once-a-second cadence,
issued in the same frame as the source blit, through the same two-phase staging download - no
stall, and nothing allocated unless the mode is on.

The analytic streaks do NOT share the extraction and need no budget: they, the anamorphic flare
and halation are directional stretch blurs whose cost is a fixed number of full-screen passes,
completely independent of how many texels cleared the threshold. Only the threshold constant is
shared. The front filter PSF is the one other sparse sprite scatter in the file and has the same
failure mode at a quarter of the candidate cells; it is deliberately untouched here and should
reuse this chain when it is done.

Left standing, and recorded: the residual is deposited AT the source rather than spread as a veil
(the bloom chain has already run by the time ExecuteStreaks is called, so there is no bloom input
left to add to); sources inside the crossfade drop their residual; the controller is one frame
behind, so a hard cut into a bright scene overshoots N for two or three frames; and there is no
blob merge, so a lamp spanning 5x5 texels spends 25 slots. decisions/s8-glare-budget.md carries
all four with the approach that answers each.

Docs: DiffractionKernelSpec.md gains 3.1 (the budget, the cost table, the three properties, the
report), the cvar list and two limitations; ConsoleReference gains r_HDRDiffractionMaxSources.

