+### 3.1 The source budget — what this pass COSTS, and the ceiling on it
+
+The sprite pass emits one quad per half-resolution texel and the vertex shader collapses the ones
+whose source is empty, so its cost is **exactly the number of texels above `HDR_Streaks_Threshold`
+times the sprite's area**. Under the scene-referred pre-exposure that threshold is an absolute
+scene value, so a bright sky, a large emissive or simply a brighter exposure can move tens of
+thousands of texels over it at once — each drawing a sprite some 160 px across, six spectral taps
+deep. At 1080p, `Reach` 0.15:
+
+| on screen | texels over threshold | half-res px blended |
+|---|---|---|
+| one practical at night | ~9 | 0.24 M |
+| a street of twenty lamps | ~200 | 5 M |
+| a pre-exposed bright sky | 50 000+ | 1 300 M |
+
+That is why the frame rate followed the brightness. It is a population problem, not a shader one:
+the physics of a broad bright region is a *veil*, and drawing it as fifty thousand overlapping
+copies of the same star buys a picture a single blur would have produced.
+
+**`r_HDRDiffractionMaxSources`** (default 256, 0 = unlimited) caps the population. Three small
+passes reduce the thresholded source to one texel holding `(cutoff, raw count, count above the
+cutoff, share of the excess below it)`; the last one corrects the cutoff from the previous
+frame's count with a multiplicative controller, and the sprite vertex shader reads that one texel:
+
+```
+w = smoothstep(cut, 2*cut, E)          one STOP of crossfade, not a hard cut
+w >= 1   full sprite, as before
+w <= 0   NO sprite - one texel carrying E * gain * (deposited glare fraction) instead
+```
+
+Three properties are load bearing:
+
+* **Energy conserving.** A source that loses its star keeps its light: the whole quantity the
+  sprite would have spread is deposited at the source's own position, in the same layer, which is
+  bilinearly upscaled into the HDR target. Turning the budget on cannot darken a scene. (It does
+  not *spread* the residual — the bloom chain has already run by the time this pass is called, so
+  there is no bloom input left to add to. A veiling pass for the residual is the next step.)
+* **No popping.** The crossfade is a stop wide, and the controller is asymmetric (fast up, slow
+  down) with a dead band at `[0.8N, N]`, so a source drifting past the budget while the camera
+  pans fades its rays over several frames.
+* **Bit-identical when it is not needed.** `raw <= N` drives the cutoff to zero, and a zero cutoff
+  is the un-budgeted vertex shader exactly. The budget is gated on `IsSceneReferredStage(5)`, so
+  off the switch the branch is never entered at all.
+
+Worst case is then `N` sprites plus one pixel per remaining source, i.e. bounded by the budget and
+`Reach` rather than by how bright the scene is.
+
+**Measuring it.** `r_HDRDiffractionDebug 3`'s once-a-second report now leads with the population,
+raw first, and the stats chain runs whenever that mode is on even with the budget at 0 — so the
+number can be read before deciding what `N` should be:
+
+```
+[BLOOM]   SOURCES: raw above threshold = 51234 of the 960 x 540 grid -- one sprite each,
+          162 x 162 half-res px per sprite = 1344.6 Mpx of blending unbudgeted
+[BLOOM]            budget 256 (r_HDRDiffractionMaxSources): 261 drew a star, cutoff 2.9 = +4.0
+          stops -- the other 50973 kept their LIGHT (12.4% of the source excess, deposited at
+          the source) and lost their shape
+```
+
+The counts are the GPU's own arithmetic over that frame's source, not an estimate, and the raw
+count uses exactly the vertex shader's collapse test.
+
+**The analytic streaks, the anamorphic flare and halation need no budget and do not have one.**
+They are directional stretch blurs: a fixed number of full-screen passes whose cost is independent
+of how many texels cleared the threshold. Only the threshold *constant* is shared. The **front
+filter PSF** is the one other sparse sprite scatter in the file and has the same failure mode at a
+quarter of the candidate cells; when it is budgeted it should reuse this chain rather than grow
+its own. Full reasoning: `scene-notes/decisions/s8-glare-budget.md`.
+
